@doc "A JavaScript value exactly as the host holds it. Ruddy never looks inside one: it is opaque, it cannot be built out of data, and forwarding it back to the host hands over the very same value, so the host's own `===` still holds. To read anything out of one, convert it with `lift` or observe it with `Host`."
type Value = ForeignValue

@doc "Why a checked conversion stopped: `path` is the position inside the value, `expected` what that position had to be, and `message` the host's own words."
type Error = ffi::DecodeError

@doc "The two directions of one checked conversion between a Ruddy type and a host value. `adapter ()` is the structural adapter for whatever type the use site infers; a specialized adapter is an ordinary record a caller writes, and is used wherever the structural one would be."
type Adapter 'a = { lower: 'a -> Result Value Error, lift: Value -> Result 'a Error }

@doc "What a host value is, as far as JavaScript itself reports. `#Null` and `#Undefined` stay apart, as do `#Array` and `#Object`; a symbol, a bigint, or a value whose class cannot be reported is `#Other`."
type Kind =
  | #Null
  | #Undefined
  | #Bool
  | #Number
  | #String
  | #Array
  | #Object
  | #Function
  | #Other

@doc "Observing a host value. Reading a property can run a getter or a proxy trap, so none of these is a pure read: they are operations of this effect, and a program that observes host values says so in its type. Every operation that can fail reports the host's own failure as an `Error` rather than throwing: a getter that raises, a proxy trap that refuses, or a value of the wrong shape comes back as `#Error`. `kind` cannot fail; a value it cannot report is `#Other`. `snapshot` copies inert data out of the host, and that copy can afterwards be read purely with `lift`; forwarding a `Value` instead keeps the host's identity."
effect Host = {
  kind: Value -> Kind,
  field: { of: Value, name: String } -> Result Value Error,
  element: { of: Value, at: Nat } -> Result Value Error,
  length: Value -> Result Nat Error,
  keys: Value -> Result [String] Error,
  snapshot: Value -> Result Value Error,
  apply: { of: Value, arguments: [Value] } -> Result Value Error,
  text: Value -> Result String Error,
}

@doc "Write a value of the inferred type as host data, or say where it could not be written. A function contract is never written: a callable is refused, and an explicit extern or callback adapter carries one instead."
let lower: 'a -> Result Value Error = ffi::encode

@doc "Read a host value as the type this use site infers, checking every position. Integers must be integral and inside the target's domain, text must really be a string, and a callable is refused: rebuilding a function from data would be a contract nobody checked, so an extern declaration owns that."
let lift: Value -> Result 'a Error = ffi::decode

@doc "The structural adapter for the type this use site infers, made from the mirror the compiler supplies there. Neither direction carries a function contract."
let adapter: () -> Adapter 'a = fn _ => { lower: lower, lift: lift }

-- Callbacks
--
-- A Ruddy function crosses to the host through an extern declaration, never through `lift`: the
-- declaration carries the complete per-arrow type, the effects the callback is allowed to perform,
-- and how the call completes.
--
--   extern each: fn(fn(Nat) -> Nat, [Nat]) -> [Nat] = "(step, values) => values.map(step)"
--
-- The callback's own effects stay in the caller's type, so the caller still handles them, and
-- completion timing grants no further permission:
--
--   extern run_with: fn(fn(Nat) -> Nat + !Log) -> Nat + !Log = "step => step(21)"
--   @async
--   extern later: fn(fn(Nat) -> Nat, Nat) -> Nat = "(step, value) => Promise.resolve(step(value))"
--
-- `@async` says the host answers with a promise, which the runtime awaits; either way the Ruddy
-- call site reads as an ordinary call. A declaration is also how a host function becomes a Ruddy
-- one, as in `extern make_adder: fn(Nat) -> fn(Nat) -> Nat`. `lift` refuses a callable, so a
-- decoder can never invent one of these contracts.

@doc "What JavaScript reports this value to be."
let kind = !Host.kind

@doc "The named property of a host value: `field \"length\" value`. The read runs whatever the host has put there."
let field = fn name of => !Host.field { of: of, name: name }

@doc "The element at an index: `element 0n value`."
let element = fn at of => !Host.element { of: of, at: at }

@doc "The `length` property, which must be a finite non-negative integer inside this target's `Nat` domain."
let length = !Host.length

@doc "The host value's own enumerable string keys, in the host's order."
let keys = !Host.keys

@doc "An inert deep copy of plain host data: objects, arrays, and primitives. A function, a value already on the path being copied, and a host object that is not plain data are all refused. The copy is data nobody else holds, so reading it afterwards with `lift` is pure."
let snapshot = !Host.snapshot

@doc "Call a host function with the given arguments: `apply [x] target`. A non-callable is refused. The call has no receiver, so read a method with `field` and let the host bind it if it needs one."
let apply = fn arguments of => !Host.apply { of: of, arguments: arguments }

@doc "Read a host string, repairing it. Ruddy text is Unicode scalar values and a host string need not be, so `lift` refuses one that is not; this is the conversion that says instead what to do about it, replacing each unpaired half with `U+FFFD`. A value that is not a string at all is still refused."
let text = !Host.text
